  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1234 PM MDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\n\nFor the CONTDVD region, especially the SW mountains...\n\nIt still is looking quite wet over the southwest mountains during \nthe long term forecast period. Persistent moist southwest flow is \ngoing to evolve through the period. The first round of precip will \nstart Sunday night and last into early Tuesday. This precip ia \nassociated with the first shortwave moving across the SW mtn region. \nThis system is rather warm and there is not too much forcing with \nit, so only expect light rain showers (with very high elevation snow \nshowers). A much more substantial trough will evolve along the \nPacific west coast and eventually move over the SW mtn region by \nlate week. Precip with this 2nd more significant system will enter \nthe SW mtns late Wednesday and likely last into the weekend. Precip \nwith this 2nd system is likely to be substantial, and we are going \nto have to be concerned with flooding over the San Juans with this \nsystem. The San Juan river basin has been running quite high from \nthe previous storm and the grounds across this region are already \nsaturated, so it is not going to take too much precip for potential \nflooding problems. Of interest, the National 0-7 day WPC QPF chart \nissued at 10:29 UTC Saturday shows that the greatest amount of \nprecip to fall over CONUS during the next 7 days will fall over the \ngreater San Juan mountains of Colorado (to be fair, some hefty QPF \namounts are also expected over the Outer Banks of NC and far south \nFlorida also). 3-4\" of liquid precip are expected. As for snow, I \ndon't think we will see impactful snow until the trough (and \nassociated colder air) gets closer towards the weekend time period. \n\n\nFor the plains...\n\nA weak boundary will push through Sunday night and this will cool \ndown the plains few degrees for Monday. Expect mainly max temps in \nthe 65-70 degree range this day with mostly partly sunny skies.\n\nFor the rest of the week, warming up with temps mainly in the 70s \nthrough the period and it will likely be dry.\n\nFor next Saturday, we should cool down as the trough moves overhead \nand we may (or may not) see some showers over the lower elevations. \n\nOverall, it is going to be a very nice week over the lower \nelevations.\n\n\nFor the Valleys and mtns adjacent to the plains... \n\nCan't rule out some spotty shower from Sunday night into mid \nweek. A much better chance of showers will develop by later \nWednesday and last into Saturday. \\/Hodanish",
